ame></persName>\n\t\t</author>\n\t\t<ptr target=\"http://arxiv.org/abs/2403.13372\" />\n\t</analytic>\n\t<monogr>\n\t\t<title level=\"m\">Proceedings of the 62nd Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics</title>\n\t\t<meeting>the 62nd Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics<address><addrLine>Bangkok, Thailand</addrLine></address></meeting>\n\t\t<imprint>\n\t\t\t<publisher>Association for Computational Linguistics</publisher>\n\t\t\t<date type=\"published\" when=\"2024\">2024</date>\n\t\t\t<biblScope unit=\"volume\">3</biblScope>\n\t\t</imprint>\n\t</monogr>\n\t<note>System Demonstrations)</note>\n</biblStruct>\n\n<biblStruct status=\"extracted\" xml:id=\"b108\">\n\t<analytic>\n\t\t<title level=\"a\" type=\"main\">Compute normalized change in entropy ∆Hi,t = N ormalize(Hi,t -H i,inintial ) for each yi 16: Compute partial sampling probability Pi,t = α + β • ∆Hi,t 17: if Pi,t &gt; τ then 18: Branch out Z additional rollouts {yi} Z i and add them to {rollouti} 19: if |yi| = M then 20: Sample {yi} until yi produce the final answer 21: else 22: Sample {yi} to produce new reasoning step 23: t ← t + 1 24: if |yi| &lt; M then 25: Sample M -|yi| additional independent rollouts and add them to {rollouti} 26: Compute rewards {ri} M i=1 for each sampled rollouts yi by running r ϕ 27: Compute Âi,t for the t-th token of oi through group relative advantage estimation. 28: for GRPO iteration = 1, . . . , µ do 29: Update the policy model π θ by maximizing the GRPO objective (Equation 6) 30: Update r ϕ through continuous training using a replay mechanism</title>\n\t\t<author>\n\t\t\t<persName><forename type=\"first\">Yifei</forename><surname>Zhou</surname></persName>\n\t\t</author>\n\t\t<author>\n\t\t\t<persName><forename type=\"first\">Andrea</forename><surname>Zanette</surname></persName>\n\t\t</author>\n\t\t<author>\n\t\t\t<persName><forename type=\"first\">Jiayi</forename><surname>Pan</surname></persName>\n\t\t</author>\n\t\t<author>\n\t\t\t<persName><forename type=\"first\">Sergey</forename><surname>Levine</surname></persName>\n\t\t</author>\n\t\t<author>\n\t\t\t<persName><forename type=\"first\">Aviral</forename><surname>Kumar</surname></persName>\n\t\t</author>\n\t\t<author>\n\t\t\t<persName><forename type=\"first\">;</forename><surname>Archer</surname></persName>\n\t\t</author>\n\t\t<author>\n\t\t\t<persName><forename type=\"first\">M</forename></persName>\n\t\t</author>\n\t\t<author>\n\t\t\t<persName><forename type=\"first\">N</forename></persName>\n\t\t</author>\n\t\t<author>\n\t\t\t<persName><forename type=\"first\">Z</forename></persName>\n\t\t</author>\n\t\t<idno type=\"arXiv\">arXiv:2402.19446</idno>\n\t</analytic>\n\t<monogr>\n\t\t<title level=\"m\">S do 5: Sample a batch D b from D 6: Update the old policy model π θ old ← π θ 7: Sample N reasoning 1-step paths {yi} N i=1 ∼ π θ old (• | q) for each question q ∈ D b 8: Compute initial entropy H i,initial of the first k tokens in each outputs 9: let all rollouts {rollouti} ← {yi} N i=1</title>\n\t\t<meeting><address><addrLine>Washington, D.C; Reed Albee of Larchmont, New York, and renamed Edward Franklin Albee III</addrLine></address></meeting>\n\t\t<imprint>\n\t\t\t<date type=\"published\" when=\"2016\">2024. Sep 16. 2016</date>\n\t\t\t<biblScope unit=\"volume\">10</biblScope>\n\t\t</imprint>\n\t</monogr>\n\t<note type=\"report_type\">arXiv preprint</note>\n\t<note>Input initial policy model π θ init reward models r ϕ ; task prompts policy model π θ ← π θ init 2: for iteration = 1 Training language model agents via hierarchical multi-turn rl I do 3: reference model π ref ← π θ 4: for step = 1 tool-call step t ← 1 11: while Any unfinished yi do 12: Parse unfinished yi, execute tools and obtain results di = T (yi) 13: Insert d into rollout yi ← yi + di 14: Generate k additional tokens based on inserted yi to compute step-level entropy Hi An example from ARPO on HLE dataset, with special symbols used in think content, search queries, Python codes, returned results and final answer highlighted with purple box , green box , blue box and red box , respectively. Example #1 from HotpotQA Dataset Question: Which playwright lived a longer life Output π θ F CASE STUDY Preprint Table B) &lt;think&gt; I need to determine which of two playwrights, Edward Albee and J. M. Barrie, lived a longer life. To do this, I will: 1. Find the birth date of Edward Albee. 2. Find the death date of Edward Albee. 3. Calculate how long Edward Albee lived. 4. Find the birth date of J. M. Barrie. 5. Find the death date of J. M. Barrie. 6. Calculate how long J. M. Barrie lived. 7. Compare the lifespans. I will use web search for steps 1-5 At the age of two weeks, he was adopted by Mr. and Mrs From an early age, . . . Page 2: Explore Edward Albee&apos;s biography, personal life, family and cause of death. Discover the real story, facts, and details of Edward Albee. Page 3: Sep 17, 2016 • Edward Albee was a Pulitzer Prize winning American playwright. His popular works include The Zoo Story, and Who&apos;s Afraid of Virginia Wolf? Albee was born Edward Harvey on March 12, 1928 in Washington, D.C. (***More search results omitted here***</note>\n</biblStruct>\n\n<biblStruct status=\"extracted\" xml:id=\"b109\">\n\t<analytic>\n\t\t<title level=\"a\" type=\"main\">Now I need to find J. M. Barrie&apos;s birth and death dates. &lt;/think&gt; ***One search step omitted here*** &lt;think&gt; From the search results:-J. M. Barrie was born on May 9, 1860</title>\n\t</analytic>\n\t<monogr>\n\t\t<title level=\"m\">Now I have both sets of dates</title>\n\t\t<meeting><address><addrLine>Albee; Born</addrLine></address></meeting>\n\t\t<imprint>\n\t\t\t<publisher>Edward</publisher>\n\t\t\t<date type=\"published\" when=\"1928-03-12\">March 12. 1928. September 16, 2016. June 19. 1937. March 12. 1928. September 16, 2016</date>\n\t\t</imprint>\n\t</monogr>\n\t<note>search results Edward Albee was born on</note>\n</biblStruct>\n\n<biblStruct status=\"extracted\" xml:id=\"b110\">\n\t<monogr>\n\t\t<title/>\n\t\t<author>\n\t\t\t<persName><forename type=\"first\">J</forename><forename type=\"middle\">M</forename><surname>Barrie</surname></persName>\n\t\t</author>\n\t\t<imprint>\n\t\t\t<date type=\"published\" when=\"1860-05-09\">May 9, 1860 -Died: June 19. 1937</date>\n\t\t\t<pubPlace>Born</pubPlace>\n\t\t</imprint>\n\t</monogr>\n</biblStruct>\n\n<biblStruct status=\"extracted\" xml:id=\"b111\">\n\t<analytic>\n\t\t<title level=\"a\" type=\"main\">Barrie: 1937 -1860 = 77 years. So, Edward Albee lived 88 years and J. M. Barrie lived 77 years. Therefore</title>\n\t</analytic>\n\t<monogr>\n\t\t<title level=\"j\">For J. M</title>\n\t\t<imprint/>\n\t</monogr>\n\t<note>For Edward Albee: 2016 -1928 = 88 years Next, I will calculate how long each playwright lived</note>\n</biblStruct>\n\n\t\t\t\t</listBibl>\n\t\t\t</div>\n\t\t</back>\n\t</text>\n</TEI>\n","pdf_hash":"2de5d4a165ed7cbae0bbe58f9bfe2f5d971904001fc0fd0e93afe3f84ddce7f8","tables":[{"table_id":"tab_0","section_path_id":"1~24","section_ordinal":41,"caption":"Table 1 :","coordinates":null,"cells":[{"row":0,"column":0,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"text":"","coordinates":null,"span":{"start":237,"end":237},"digest":"b342c83d7060c5852f741e8e009d375103df2e1f7c76fbc512e575cd86d7b03d"},{"row":0,"column":1,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"text":"","coordinates":null,"span":{"start":237,"end":237},"digest":"7e8d5b72d290ba59f0edbe3d6f961eea1a534c250a6ef2cac5b85c0c2111735f"},{"row":0,"column":2,"colspan":3,"rowspan":1,"text":"Mathematical Reasoning","coordinates":null,"span":{"start":237,"end":259},"digest":"27a378e6f2587ac6270c4888126fc03a0f3eef1bfbe167bcf03d0fe66fa7b2e0"},{"row":0,"column":5,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"text":"","coordinates":null,"span":{"start":259,"end":259},"digest":"eb71df8bab690d4fb0739206468d7b153927f36e2c66221a63e5a901a3018e45"},{"row":0,"column":6,"colspan":5,"rowspan":1,"text":"Knowledge-Intensive Reasoning","coordinates":null,"span":{"start":259,"end":288},"digest":"2f